<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  authorSlug={authorSlug}
  pubDate={pubDate}
  readingTime={readingTime}
  image={image}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  headings={headings}
  category="SaaS"
  categorySlug="tools"
  subcategorySlug="saas"
>
  <h2 id="introduction">Forty-Seven Hours of Video Calls Last Month</h2>
  <p>
    Counted them. Forty-seven hours staring at faces in little boxes. That's my actual calendar from November.
    <a href="/guides/how-to-run-effective-remote-meetings">Daily standups</a>, client presentations, the occasional
    all-hands that could've been an email.
  </p>
  <p>
    Point is: your video tool matters. A lot. Laggy connection during a pitch? Lost the client. Zoom bombing in
    2020? That was fun. Audio cutting out every thirty seconds? Everyone hates you now.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spent over forty hours testing ten different platforms. Zoom, Meet, Teams, plus some newer ones like Around
    and Whereby. Ran actual <a href="/guides/remote-team-collaboration-best-practices">team meetings</a>, recorded
    podcasts, hosted a webinar with 200 people. Measured video quality at different bandwidths. Timed how long
    it takes guests to join without downloading anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Short version:</strong>
    <strong>Zoom</strong> for most teams. <strong>Google Meet</strong> if you're
    already in Google Workspace.
    <strong>Teams</strong> if you use Microsoft stuff. <strong>Whereby</strong> if
    you want dead simple.
    <strong>Loom</strong> for async.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-picks">
    Quick Picks: Best Video Conferencing Tools at a Glance
  </h2>
  <ComparisonTable
    columns={quickPicksColumns}
    rows={quickPicksRows}
    caption="Quick comparison of top video conferencing tools"
  />

  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What to Look For</h2>
  <p>Before getting into specific tools, here's what actually matters:</p>

  <h3>Video and Audio Quality</h3>
  <p>
    Most important thing. HD video, good audio leveling. Most modern platforms
    handle this fine. But it depends on your internet too. A good <a
      href="/gear/best-webcams-video-calls">webcam</a
    > and <a href="/gear/best-microphones-remote-meetings">microphone</a> make a
    huge difference.
  </p>

  <h3>Participant Limits</h3>
  <p>
    How many people can join? Free plans usually cap at a hundred. Paid goes
    from three hundred up to ten thousand for webinars. Think about your biggest
    meetings.
  </p>

  <h3>Time Limits</h3>
  <p>
    Free plans often limit group calls to forty or sixty minutes. Fine for quick
    syncs. Annoying for workshops or long client calls.
  </p>

  <h3>Recording</h3>
  <p>
    Can you record? Where do recordings save. Cloud or local? Who can access
    them? Matters for async teams and documentation.
  </p>

  <h3>Screen Sharing</h3>
  <p>
    Everyone offers basic screen sharing. Look for multiple shares, annotation
    tools, remote control, whiteboarding.
  </p>

  <h3>Integrations</h3>
  <p>
    Works with your calendar? Google Calendar, Outlook. That's essential. <a
      href="/blog/best-team-communication-tools">Slack</a
    >,
    <a href="/tools/best-project-management-software"
      >project management tools</a
    >, CRM stuff. Bonuses.
  </p>

  <h3>Security</h3>
  <p>
    End-to-end encryption. Waiting rooms. Password protection. Ability to lock
    meetings. HIPAA and SOC two compliance if you're in healthcare or finance.
  </p>

  <h3>Ease of Use</h3>
  <p>
    Can guests join without accounts? Intuitive interface? Mobile apps? Easier
    for guests means less friction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">
    Detailed Reviews: Top 10 Video Conferencing Tools
  </h2>

  <h3 id="zoom">1. Zoom</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/zoom"
    text="Try Zoom Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="zoom"
  />

  <p>
    Free with forty-minute limit. Pro is sixteen bucks per host monthly.
    Business is twenty.
  </p>
  <p>Free plan: up to a hundred people</p>
  <p>Paid: up to a thousand (or ten thousand-plus with Zoom Webinar)</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Rock-solid reliability. Connections stay stable.</li>
    <li>Tons of features. Breakout rooms, polling, whiteboard, reactions.</li>
    <li>Great for webinars. Registration, Q&A, panelist controls.</li>
    <li>Virtual backgrounds are best-in-class.</li>
    <li>Recording. Local on free, cloud on paid.</li>
    <li>Strong mobile apps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Forty-minute cap on free is disruptive.</li>
    <li>Breakout rooms need paid plan.</li>
    <li>Some people are burned out on Zoom specifically.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Teams needing reliable video with advanced features. Agencies, consultants,
    educators, webinar hosts.
  </p>

  <h3 id="google-meet">2. Google Meet</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/google-meet"
    text="Try Google Meet"
    variant="primary"
    program="google-meet"
  />

  <p>
    Free with sixty-minute limit. Workspace Individual is eight bucks monthly.
    Business is twelve per user.
  </p>
  <p>Free plan: up to a hundred</p>
  <p>Paid: up to five hundred on Enterprise</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Seamless Google integration. Gmail, Calendar, Drive. Just works.</li>
    <li>No app needed. Runs in Chrome.</li>
    <li>Sixty-minute free meetings beats Zoom's forty.</li>
    <li>Clean, simple interface.</li>
    <li>Mobile apps don't kill your battery.</li>
    <li>Live captions built in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Recording needs paid plan.</li>
    <li>Limited features. No whiteboarding, fewer integrations.</li>
    <li>Breakout rooms paid-only and less intuitive than Zoom's.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Teams already using Google Workspace. If your email and calendar are Google,
    Meet makes sense.
  </p>

  <h3 id="microsoft-teams">3. Microsoft Teams</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/microsoft-teams"
    text="Try Teams Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="microsoft-teams"
  />

  <p>
    Free with sixty-minute limit. Essentials is four bucks per user monthly.
    Business is twelve-fifty.
  </p>
  <p>Free plan: up to a hundred</p>
  <p>Paid: up to ten thousand</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Most generous free plan. Recording, breakout rooms, sixty minutes. All
      free.
    </li>
    <li>All-in-one. Video, chat, files, project management.</li>
    <li>Microsoft 365 integration. Outlook, OneDrive, Office.</li>
    <li>Can handle up to ten thousand people on enterprise plans.</li>
    <li>Good security.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Interface is cluttered. Too much going on for simple calls.</li>
    <li>Resource-heavy. Uses more CPU and RAM than lighter options.</li>
    <li>External guests get confused. Better for internal teams.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Organizations using Microsoft 365. Larger companies. Teams wanting
    all-in-one. Budget-conscious teams. Free tier is generous.
  </p>

  <h3 id="webex">4. Webex</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/webex"
    text="Try Webex Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="webex"
  />

  <p>
    Free with forty-minute limit. Starter is fourteen-fifty per host monthly.
    Business is thirty.
  </p>
  <p>Free plan: up to a hundred</p>
  <p>Paid: up to a thousand</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Enterprise security. End-to-end encryption, SOC two, HIPAA.</li>
    <li>Huge webinars. Supports up to a hundred thousand people.</li>
    <li>Cisco reliability. Solid infrastructure.</li>
    <li>AI features. Noise removal, gesture recognition.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Interface feels dated.</li>
    <li>Expensive for small teams. Geared toward enterprise budgets.</li>
    <li>Learning curve is steep.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Large enterprises with strict security needs. Think healthcare, finance,
    government sectors.
  </p>

  <h3 id="gotomeeting">5. GoToMeeting</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/gotomeeting"
    text="Try GoToMeeting"
    variant="primary"
    program="gotomeeting"
  />

  <p>
    Professional is fourteen bucks per organizer monthly. Business is nineteen.
    No free plan, just fourteen-day trial.
  </p>
  <p>Up to two hundred fifty participants.</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Professional webinar stuff. Registration, polls, handouts, analytics.
    </li>
    <li>Good drawing tools. Better annotation than most.</li>
    <li>Personal meeting rooms. Same URL every time.</li>
    <li>Solid mobile apps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>No free plan.</li>
    <li>More expensive than Zoom for similar features.</li>
    <li>Branding feels dated.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Consultants and coaches running frequent webinars use this.</p>

  <h3 id="around">6. Around</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/around"
    text="Try Around"
    variant="primary"
    program="around"
  />

  <p>
    Pro is ten bucks per user monthly. Business is sixteen. No free plan,
    fourteen-day trial.
  </p>
  <p>Up to fifty participants.</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Floating video bubbles. Not full-screen takeover.</li>
    <li>Everyone can share screens at once.</li>
    <li>Low resource usage. Less CPU than Zoom.</li>
    <li>Beautiful, modern design.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>No free plan.</li>
    <li>Limited to fifty people.</li>
    <li>Missing features. No recording, breakout rooms, webinar stuff.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Small, design-focused teams that want casual, less invasive video.</p>

  <h3 id="whereby">7. Whereby</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/whereby"
    text="Try Whereby Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="whereby"
  />

  <p>
    Free for four participants. Pro is ten bucks per host monthly. Business is
    sixteen.
  </p>
  <p>Free plan: four people</p>
  <p>Paid: up to two hundred</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>No downloads. Runs in browser.</li>
    <li>Custom URLs. Get whereby. Com/yourname permanently.</li>
    <li>Beautiful interface. Clean, minimal.</li>
    <li>Embed in websites.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Free plan only four people. Very limited.</li>
    <li>Recording only on Business plan.</li>
    <li>Fewer features than competitors.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One-on-ones and small meetings. Therapists, coaches. Anyone wanting simplest
    possible video.
  </p>

  <h3 id="discord">8. Discord</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/discord"
    text="Try Discord Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="discord"
  />

  <p>Free. Nitro is ten bucks monthly for perks.</p>
  <p>Up to fifty in video. Unlimited voice.</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Totally free. No time limits, no caps on voice.</li>
    <li>Always-on channels. Drop-in collaboration.</li>
    <li>
      Great for gaming and creative teams. Screen share with audio works well.
    </li>
    <li>Community features. Forums, roles.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Doesn't look professional. Gaming branding doesn't suit corporate clients.
    </li>
    <li>No native recording. Need bots or third-party stuff.</li>
    <li>Video capped at fifty. Voice unlimited though.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Creative teams, game studios, developer communities. Anyone wanting
    persistent voice channels.
  </p>

  <h3 id="loom">9. Loom</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/loom"
    text="Try Loom Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="loom"
  />

  <p>
    Free for twenty-five videos, five minutes each. Business is twelve-fifty per
    creator monthly.
  </p>
  <p>Async video messages, not live calls.</p>

  <h4>What works:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Perfect for async. Record and share instead of meeting.</li>
    <li>Screen plus camera. Your face in a circle.</li>
    <li>Instant links. Viewers don't download anything.</li>
    <li>Comments and reactions with timestamps.</li>
    <li>Fast and lightweight.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What's annoying:</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Not for live calls. Recording only.</li>
    <li>Free plan limited. Five-minute videos, twenty-five total.</li>
    <li>Can feel impersonal. One-way.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Async teams use this. Product demos, customer support, code reviews.
    Reducing meeting overhead.
  </p>

  <h3 id="riverside">10. Riverside</h3>
