  <h2 id="free-vs-paid">Free or Paid?</h2>
  <p>When to stick with free:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Meetings under forty to sixty minutes</li>
    <li>Fewer than a hundred participants</li>
    <li>Don't need cloud recording</li>
    <li>Breakout rooms not essential</li>
  </ul>

  <p>When to upgrade:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Run long meetings. Training, workshops, client calls over an hour.</li>
    <li>Need recording. Compliance, training, documentation.</li>
    <li>Use breakout rooms. Workshops, education, collaboration.</li>
    <li>Have large meetings. Over a hundred regularly.</li>
    <li>Run webinars. Registration, Q&A, analytics need paid plans.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Best value paid plans:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Zoom Pro at sixteen bucks per host monthly. Best features for the price.
    </li>
    <li>
      Microsoft Teams Essentials at four per user monthly. Cheapest with good
      features.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google Workspace Individual at eight monthly. Great if you need other
      Google tools.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="integration-capabilities">Integrations</h2>

  <h3>Calendar:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Google Meet. Native Google Calendar integration.</li>
    <li>Microsoft Teams. Native Outlook.</li>
    <li>
      Zoom. Works with Google Calendar, Outlook, most calendars via plugins.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Slack:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Zoom. Start meetings from Slack with /zoom command.</li>
    <li>Google Meet. Start calls with /meet.</li>
    <li>Whereby. One-click meeting links.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Project Management:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Zoom. Integrates with Asana, Monday, Trello.</li>
    <li>Microsoft Teams. Deep integration with Microsoft Planner.</li>
    <li>Loom embeds videos in Notion, ClickUp, Jira.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="security-features">Security</h2>

  <h3>Encryption stuff:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Zoom offers it, but you gotta manually enable.</li>
    <li>Whereby does E2EE for all calls.</li>
    <li>Most others use TLS encryption. Secure but not E2EE.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Security Controls:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Waiting rooms. All major platforms have this.</li>
    <li>Password protection. Standard everywhere.</li>
    <li>Lock meetings. Prevent new joiners mid-call.</li>
    <li>Remove participants. Host controls.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Compliance:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>HIPAA compliant: Zoom, Webex, Google Meet (Workspace only), Teams.</li>
    <li>SOC two certified. All major platforms.</li>
    <li>GDPR compliant. All major platforms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

  <h2 id="final-recommendations">Which One?</h2>

  <h3>For most teams: Zoom</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Zoom</strong> remains gold standard. Reliable, feature-rich, everyone
    knows how to use it. Forty-minute limit on free is annoying, but <strong
      >Zoom Pro at sixteen bucks per host monthly</strong
    > is worth it for most professional teams.
  </p>

  <h3>If you use Google Workspace: Google Meet</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Google Meet</strong> is obvious. Integration with Gmail and Google Calendar
    just works. Simpler than Zoom. Generous sixty-minute free tier.
  </p>

  <h3>If you use Microsoft 365: Microsoft Teams</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> offers incredible value, especially on free
    plan. If you're already paying for Microsoft 365, Teams is included and beats
    most competitors' paid plans.
  </p>

  <h3>If you want simplicity: Whereby</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Whereby</strong> can't be beat for small teams, coaches, anyone wanting
    zero-friction video calls. No downloads, beautiful interface, custom URLs.
  </p>

  <h3>If you run webinars: Zoom or GoToMeeting</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Zoom</strong> or <strong>GoToMeeting</strong> both excel at webinars. Registration,
    Q&A, polls, analytics. Zoom is more popular. GoToMeeting has better drawing tools.
  </p>

  <h3>If you want async: Loom</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Loom</strong> isn't a Zoom replacement. It's a meeting reducer. Use it
    to record updates, demos, feedback instead of scheduling yet another call. Your
    team will thank you.
  </p>

  <h3>If you're budget-conscious: Discord or Microsoft Teams Free</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Discord</strong> is totally free with unlimited calling. Fifty video participants.
    <strong>Microsoft Teams Free</strong> offers sixty-minute meetings with recording
    and breakout rooms.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8">
    Bottom line: Can't go wrong with <strong>Zoom</strong>, <strong
      >Google Meet</strong
    >, or <strong>Teams</strong>. Choose based on which ecosystem you're already
    using. For most independent teams, <strong>Zoom Pro</strong> is the best investment.
  </p>

